A48 -- RECORD AUDIT: A33 section 5's first integral vs the baryon-only SPARC fits
======================================================================================================================
K = 4 pi G = 8.387173e-10 ; f_b = 0.157126 ; M_c/M_b = 5.364327 ; M_src/M_b = 6.364327 ; sqrt = 2.522762
a0(Lambda=1.0890e-52) = 9.354173e-11 ; canonical = 9.360300e-11 ; record = 1.132000e-10 ; c sqrt(G rho_DE) = 1.870835e-10

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART A -- what the first integral is, and what it is not
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A33 section 5, verbatim (MASTER_ACTION.md:278-280):
  "Consistency of the three: (L) differentiated and (P-sph) substituted give
   (r^2 g)' = K rho_src r^2 + (r^2 u)', i.e. g = g_N + u with g_N = G M_src/r^2, which is
   (P-sph)'s first integral.  That is why the law is exact and not an expansion, and why
   the P sector is a charge sector."
A33 section 3.1, verbatim: 'rho = rho_m + rho_c is the total matter density, d = D_i P^i.'

A1    PASS  lapse-EL minus printed (L): residual 0                     | 0
      (L) IS the lapse equation of A33's own action -- the equation is not where the defect sits
A2    PASS  O(eps) lapse eq residual: 0                                | 0
      lap(Phi) = K(rho + d) = 4 pi G (rho + D.P); Lambda is absorbed by the background
A3    PASS  Gauss vs first-integral form: max rel 3.20e-04 (3 radii)   | < 5e-3
      g = G M_rho/r^2 + u is Gauss' law applied to A2: an identity, no constraint on M_rho
A4    PASS  (g - u) - g^2/(g+a0) with u = a0 g/(g+a0): 0               | 0
      the ONLY content is 'g_N is the Newtonian field of the source'; WHICH mass is not in it
A5    PASS  all-orders residual / term = 0.221                         | > 0.02 means 'not an identity'
      §5 invokes only (L) and (P); beyond O(eps) those two do NOT imply the identity (the f-equation, which §5 does not use, would be needed).  Every galactic use is at O(eps), so no number moves.
A6    PASS  S_printed - S_partition = rho_c*varsigma                   | varsigma rho_c
      the printed (L) counts varsigma*rho_c twice: the charge's share sits in rho_c AND in d
      A33 section 1: rho_c = varsigma rho_c (charge) + (1-varsigma) rho_c (native).  The
      first integral then gives M_src = M_b + (1-varsigma) M_c, and the law's amplitude is
      sqrt(M_src/M_b):
        varsigma     M_src/M_b     deep-MOND amplitude vs baryons
        0.0000         6.3643        2.5228
        0.4716         3.8345        1.9582
        0.9865         1.0724        1.0356
        1.0000         1.0000        1.0000
A6b   PASS  varsigma=0 -> M_src=6.3643 M_b (amp 2.523); varsigma=1 -> M_src=1.0000 M_b (amp 1.000) | 6.3643/2.523 ; 1.0000/1.0000
      A40's branch is the varsigma=0 end; A33 section 7.3 forces varsigma=1, i.e. M_src = M_b
      so 'M_src = M_b + M_c' is not a derivation -- it is the varsigma -> 0 end of A33's own
      one-parameter family, the end at which the P charge is switched off.  The record's own
      section 7.3 selects varsigma = 1 (a constant partition deforms the law by 0.243 at
      g = a0/2 and is excluded by the RAR), which is M_src = M_b.
A7    PASS  deep limit (g_N - g) -> -2*a0                              | -2 a0
      M_src = M_b + M_ph gives g = g_N + 2u, i.e. Newtonian + 2a0, NOT MOND: not a reading of the law
A8    PASS  amplitude 2.522762 ; a0 factor 6.364327 ; Lambda factor 40.5047 | 2.52276 ; 6.36433 ; 40.504
      g ~ sqrt(a0 g_N): a 6.364x source is a 2.523x amplitude, absorbed only by a0/6.3643
      the brief's 'a0 must be 2.523x smaller' is one factor of 2.523 short: a0 enters as
      sqrt(a0 g_N), so the a0 correction is 2.523^2 = 6.3643.  All numbers below use 6.3643.

